TI  - Expert statement: pneumothorax associated with endoscopic valve therapy for 
      emphysema--potential mechanisms, treatment algorithm, and case examples.
PG  - 513-21
LID - 10.1159/000360642 [doi]
AB  - The use of endoscopically placed unidirectional valves for the treatment of 
      emphysema is increasing. With better patient selection, there is also an 
      increased likelihood of complications associated with the procedure, such as 
      postprocedural pneumothorax. There is, however, little evidence of pneumothorax 
      management in patients with severe COPD and emphysema. This report describes an 
      expert recommendation that has been developed to outline pneumothorax management 
      after valve placement to inform physicians and patients of the risk-benefit 
      profile and to assist them in decision making. Skilled and aggressive 
      pneumothorax management is necessary in this patient population, and by following 
      these recommendations traumatic scenarios, prolonged drainage, extended 
      hospitalizations, and/or surgery might be avoided in many cases.
