TI  - Bronchoscopic and nonbronchoscopic methods of airway culturing in tracheostomized 
      children.
PG  - 582-7
LID - 10.4187/respcare.02483 [doi]
AB  - INTRODUCTION: Distal airway secretions can be sampled by bronchoscopic 
      bronchoalveolar lavage (B-BAL), blind protected BAL (BP-BAL), and tracheal 
      aspiration (TA). We quantitatively compared the cultures of distal airway 
      secretions from BP-BAL, B-BAL, and TA specimens, and assessed the efficacy of the 
      three above methods in diagnosing bronchitis in tracheostomized children. 
      METHODS: Twenty children with tracheostomies underwent BP-BAL, B-BAL, and TA. 
      Samples were sent for quantitative bacterial cultures. The diagnosis of 
      bronchitis was made based on a validated visual grading system as well as on 
      positive quantitative cultures from the BAL fluid. Diagnostic agreement between 
      cultures obtained by the three methods and the visual grading scores was 
      determined by kappa statistics. RESULTS: The diagnosis of bronchitis by visual 
      grading score had substantial agreement with BP-BAL, moderate agreement with 
      B-BAL, and fair agreement with TA results. BP-BAL specimens had significantly 
      lower pathogenic colonies (P < .05) than either B-BAL or TA specimens. 
      CONCLUSIONS: BP-BAL allows for more accurate sampling of lower airway secretions 
      in tracheostomized children and is more accurate in the diagnosis of bronchitis 
      in this group.
