VI  - 128 Suppl 1
DP  - 2014 Jan
TI  - Novel use of Coblation technology in an unusual congenital tracheal stenosis.
PG  - S55-8
LID - 10.1017/S0022215113000996 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: We report the case of an unusual late presentation of congenital 
      tracheal stenosis in a 13-year-old boy. He was treated with minimally invasive 
      Coblation resection of the stenotic segment, avoiding a major open tracheal 
      resection and reconstruction. This case report is the first to document the use 
      of an ultra-fine Coblation wand in the treatment of congenital tracheal stenosis. 
      RESULTS: The case proceeded well, without any complications. The patient had a 
      fully healed and patent trachea at 12-week post-operative review. CONCLUSION: 
      Complex cases of congenital stenosis should be managed with a multidisciplinary 
      approach. Different and novel treatment options should be explored to find one 
      that suits the individual patient. Minimally invasive Coblation technology can 
      offer less invasive treatment with quicker recovery and shorter hospitalisation.
