DP  - 2012 Sep-Oct
TI  - Bronchoscopic management of a rare benign endobronchial tumor.
PG  - 251-4
LID - 10.1016/j.rppneu.2012.02.003 [doi]
AB  - Benign endobronchial tumors are uncommon. Bronchoscopic removal is the preferred 
      modality of treatment although surgery may be required in some cases. Rigid 
      bronchoscopy is usually recommended in the management of these tumors. However, 
      flexible bronchoscopy is also used in many centers. We present a case of 
      endobronchial lipoma, where an unusual complication during flexible bronchoscopic 
      resection using snare forceps necessitated urgent rigid bronchoscopy. This case 
      highlights the importance of rigid bronchoscopy in the management of 
      endobronchial tumors. We believe that with a large benign endobronchial tumor in 
      tracheal or main-stem bronchus, physicians should initially employ rigid 
      bronchoscopy, switching to flexible if more peripheral treatment is required.
