TI  - Salvaging the dehisced lung transplant bronchial anastomosis with homograft 
      aorta.
PG  - 666-8
LID - 10.1510/icvts.2011.269910 [doi]
AB  - This is a case of 50-year-old male who underwent left single lung transplantation 
      for pulmonary fibrosis. He sustained a bronchial dehiscence with a pulmonary 
      artery-bronchial fistula which was primarily repaired. One week later, there was 
      complete bronchial dehiscence followed by a massive hemoptysis. At operation, 
      following resection of necrotic donor bronchus there was a sizeable gap between 
      donor and recipient bronchus, which was bridged with a cryopreserved aortic 
      homograft. The homograft patch provided a satisfactory repair without malacia. 
      The patient required retransplantation six months later for reasons unassociated 
      with the repair. Homograft aorta proved useful material for salvaging the 
      dehisced lung transplant bronchial anastomosis.
