VI  - 67
IP  - 2
DP  - 2011 Apr
TI  - [Bifocal lung cancer: the same histology?].
PG  - 113-7
LID - 10.1016/j.pneumo.2009.12.015 [doi]
AB  - Whereas synchronous lung cancer is rare, synchronous small cell lung cancer 
      (SCLC) and non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) are exceptional. The authors report 
      the case of a 61-year-old man with synchronous unilateral adenocarcinoma and 
      small cell lung cancer, raising the question as to the need for the histology of 
      all of the lesions in the same lobe or same lung as well as the treatment. The 
      medical history, biology, CT and (18)F-FDG TEP-CT did not support a diagnosis of 
      synchronous lung cancer. The prognosis was poor and only surgery could improve 
      the prognosis. This is a rare case and illustrates the difficulty in the 
      diagnosis of multiple lung cancer and the difficulty in treating synchronous lung 
      cancer with different histologies (SCLC and NSCLC).
