DP  - 2011 Jun
TI  - Conservative treatment of severe tracheal laceration after endotracheal 
      intubation.
PG  - 861-2
LID - 10.4187/respcare.00891 [doi]
AB  - Endotracheal intubation can lead to iatrogenic trauma to the upper airways. 
      Superficial mucosal tears in the mouth, pharynx, or larynx are common and can 
      cause secondary infection and acute respiratory distress due to massive air leak. 
      We report a patient who sustained a severe 5-cm tracheal laceration and 
      subcutaneous emphysema after intubation. She was successfully weaned from the 
      ventilator after adjustment of the endotracheal cuff, therapy with broad-spectrum 
      antibiotics, and continuous airway humidification.
