IS  - 1841-8724 (Print)
IS  - 1841-8724 (Linking)
VI  - 19
IP  - 1
DP  - 2010 Mar
TI  - State-of-the art endoscopic imaging in lung cancer: should specialties collide or 
      concur?
PG  - 93-7
AB  - Endoscopic ultrasound has been recently established as a routine diagnostic and 
      staging procedure in lung cancer patients, mainly because of the possibility of 
      tissue sampling. Transesophageal endoscopic ultrasound-guided fine needle 
      aspiration (EUS-FNA) represents a method which not only allows the visualization 
      of the upper gastrointestinal tract, but also offers good visualisation of the 
      posterior and inferior mediastinum. Endobronchial ultrasound-guided 
      transbronchial needle aspiration (EBUS-TBNA) permits the assessment of the tumors 
      in the anterior mediastinum. Based on previously published literature, it is 
      obvious that this minimally invasive procedure now offers the possibility of 
      tissue diagnosis in mediastinal masses including primary tumours or secondary 
      lesions from both benign and malignant diseases.
