TI  - Risk factors associated with hypoxemia during foreign body removal from airways 
      in childhood.
PG  - 986-9
LID - S0165-5876(13)00126-2 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.ijporl.2013.03.026 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVE: The aim of this study was to analyze the association between variables 
      associated with hypoxemia in children who underwent rigid bronchoscopy for 
      foreign body removal. METHODS: From April 1993 to April 2011, four hundred and 
      one children who inhaled foreign bodies were included. Apart from descriptive 
      statistics, univariate and multivariate analyses were performed to identify risk 
      factors related to hypoxemia. RESULTS: Among the patients aged up to one year, 
      the risk of hypoxemia was five and a half times higher than for patients aged 1 
      or older (OR=5.6), whereas the risk of patients who underwent foreign body 
      removal using seed type tweezers having hypoxemia was approximately 4 times 
      higher than that of patients who underwent this procedure with other types of 
      tweezers (OR=3.7). Furthermore, for each additional minute in the duration of the 
      procedure, the risk of hypoxemia reached 4% (OR=1.04). CONCLUSION: Our results 
      suggest that children younger than 1 year who require RB seem to be vulnerable to 
      a higher risk of hypoxemia, especially in longer procedures in which seed 
      tweezers are used.
