IS  - 0918-2918 (Linking)
VI  - 51
IP  - 11
DP  - 2012
TI  - A case of Nocardia asteroides infection in a patient with HIV/AIDS diagnosed by 
      endobronchial ultrasound-guided transbronchial needle aspiration (EBUS-TBNA).
PG  - 1413-7
AB  - We report a 45-year-old man with HIV/AIDS who developed mediastinal 
      lymphadenopathy caused by Nocardia asteroides infection that was diagnosed by 
      endobronchial ultrasound-guided transbronchial needle aspiration (EBUS-TBNA). He 
      was an untreated HIV-infected man who was admitted to our hospital because of 
      Pneumocystis pneumonia and Cytomegalovirus pneumonia. After treatment for 
      pneumonia, cough and fever recurred and chest computed tomography revealed 
      subcarinal lymphadenopathy with rim enhancement. To identify the etiology, we 
      performed EBUS-TBNA and obtained purulent exudates which contained N. asteroides. 
      EBUS-TBNA is a useful and safe technique for the diagnosis of mediastinal 
      infectious lymphadenopathy of unknown origin.
