TI  - Diffuse alveolar hemorrhage as a complication of dual antiplatelet therapy for 
      acute coronary syndrome.
PG  - 407-11
LID - 10.1016/j.carrev.2011.03.003 [doi]
AB  - Diffuse alveolar hemorrhage after percutaneous coronary intervention is a rare 
      complication that has been mostly reported in association with glycoprotein 
      IIb/IIIa inhibitors. We herein report a case of a 64-year-old male who developed 
      a diffuse pulmonary hemorrhage after coronary stent implantation for ST-elevation 
      myocardial infarction. Diagnostic bronchoscopy determined the hemorrhage to be a 
      bland pulmonary hemorrhage, and this pattern suggested that combination therapy 
      with aspirin and ticlopidine was therefore the most likely cause. The combination 
      of aspirin and thienopyridine agents is a routine therapeutic protocol for 
      patients after coronary stent implantation. Therefore, physicians should be aware 
      that this dual antiplatelet therapy might sometimes induce fatal complications.
