TI  - Noninvasive ventilation and lung volume reduction.
PG  - 251-69
LID - S0272-5231(13)00166-4 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.ccm.2013.10.011 [doi]
AB  - As parenchymal lung disease in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease becomes 
      increasingly severe there is a diminishing prospect of drug therapies conferring 
      clinically useful benefit. Lung volume reduction surgery is effective in patients 
      with heterogenous upper zone emphysema and reduced exercise tolerance, and is 
      probably underused. Rapid progress is being made in nonsurgical approaches to 
      lung volume reduction, but use outside specialized centers cannot be recommended 
      presently. Noninvasive ventilation given to patients with acute hypercapnic 
      exacerbation of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease reduces mortality and 
      morbidity, but the place of chronic non-invasive ventilatory support remains more 
      controversial.
