TI  - Occult laryngomalacia resulting in obstructive sleep apnea in an infant.
PG  - 1617-9
LID - S0165-5876(13)00339-X [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.ijporl.2013.07.006 [doi]
AB  - Classic laryngomalacia presents in the awake infant with progressive stridor when 
      agitated. Occult laryngomalacia usually presents with stridor in children older 
      than 2 years and is limited to sleep or exercise. There have been no documented 
      cases of occult laryngomalacia causing obstructive sleep apnea in infants. We 
      report the youngest documented case of an infant with state-dependent 
      laryngomalacia resulting in severe obstructive sleep apnea. This patient was 
      successfully treated with supraglottoplasty, with resolution of symptoms. In 
      conclusion, state-dependent laryngomalacia resulting in obstructive sleep apnea 
      may present in children younger than 12 months of age. In these individuals, 
      supraglottoplasty should be considered.
