TI  - Bronchial mucous gland adenoma revealed following acute pneumonia.
PG  - 347-9
LID - 10.1093/icvts/ivr104 [doi]
AB  - A 54-year old male, current smoker, was admitted to the emergency unit with 
      lingular pneumonia. The follow-up chest CT and bronchoscopy showed an 
      airway-blocking intrabronchial tumour. After surgical resection, pathological 
      examination established the diagnosis of a bronchial mucous gland adenoma. The 
      bronchial mucous gland adenoma is an extremely rare and benign lung tumour. It is 
      composed of mucus-containing acini lined with cuboidal cells without 
      pleomorphism. Total surgical resection is usually required for complete diagnosis 
      and treatment. The main differential diagnoses are a low-grade mucoepidermoid 
      carcinoma and a mucinous cystadenoma of the lung. This case highlights the 
      importance of a complete lung workup after acute pneumonia in patients with a 
      history of smoking, including the CT scan and bronchoscopy.
