DP  - 2010 Apr
TI  - Bronchial thermoplasty: a novel technique in the treatment of severe asthma.
PG  - 101-16
LID - 10.1177/1753465810367505 [doi]
AB  - New therapies are needed for patients with severe persistent asthma who cannot 
      achieve control with current therapy of inhaled corticosteroids and long-acting 
      beta(2)-agonists. Bronchial thermoplasty is a novel intervention for asthma that 
      delivers controlled thermal energy to the airway wall during a series of 
      bronchoscopies, resulting in a prolonged reduction in airway smooth muscle mass. 
      We review the method of performing bronchial thermoplasty with the Alair System, 
      how to appropriately select and manage patients undergoing bronchial 
      thermoplasty, and the clinical experience to date with this treatment. 
      Randomized, controlled clinical trials with bronchial thermoplasty in subjects 
      with severe asthma have resulted in improvements in overall asthma control as 
      demonstrated by significant improvement in quality of life, asthma symptoms, 
      severe exacerbations requiring corticosteroids, days lost from work/school/other 
      daily activities due to asthma, and healthcare utilization.
