TI  - Dexmedetomidine and propofol total intravenous anesthesia for airway foreign body 
      removal.
PG  - 481-4
LID - 10.1007/s11845-014-1105-4 [doi]
AB  - PURPOSE: To detail our experience using dexmedetomidine in combination with 
      propofol for airway foreign body removal in spontaneously breathing patients. 
      CLINICAL FEATURES: Dexmedetomidine and propofol intravenous anesthesia as a 
      primary anesthetic was used for three pediatric patients with severe respiratory 
      impairment due to foreign body aspiration and two elderly patients requiring 
      airway foreign body removal by rigid bronchoscopy. All patients were 
      spontaneously ventilating, and had successful airway foreign body removal without 
      severe hypoxemia. The three pediatric patients maintained stable respiratory and 
      hemodynamic profiles. However, dexmedetomidine caused a significant change in the 
      hemodynamics of the elderly patients. CONCLUSION: Dexmedetomidine and propofol 
      intravenous anesthesia provided good anesthesia without causing respiratory 
      depression. However, this technique related to more hemodynamic depression in 
      elderly patients than in pediatrics.
