VI  - 59
IP  - 3
DP  - 2014 Mar
TI  - Broncholithiasis secondary to pulmonary actinomycosis.
PG  - e27-30
LID - 10.4187/respcare.02370 [doi]
AB  - We report a case of broncholithiasis secondary to pulmonary actinomycosis. 
      Broncholithiasis has occasionally been reported in association with 
      actinomycosis, but it is unclear if actinomycosis leads to lymph node 
      calcification with subsequent erosion into the airway, producing a broncholith, 
      or if an existing generic broncholith is secondarily colonized with Actinomyces. 
      The patient had post-obstruction pneumonia, and computed tomography showed 
      calcified nodules obstructing the bronchus intermedius and distal necrotizing 
      pneumonia. The nodules included sulfur granules containing Gram-positive 
      branching filamentous organisms consistent with Actinomyces. The finding of 
      Actinomyces throughout the broncholith is strong evidence that the etiology of 
      the broncholithiasis was a primary pulmonary Actinomyces infection.
