TI  - Bilateral pneumothorax after bronchoscopy without biopsy--a rare complication: 
      case presentation and literature review.
PG  - 57-60
LID - 10.1097/LBR.0b013e3182426243 [doi]
AB  - Bronchoscopy and bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) are widely accepted diagnostic 
      procedures in various pulmonary etiologies. Complications of bronchoscopy are 
      relatively infrequent and most often minor, namely, bleeding and infection. 
      Pneumothorax is a rare complication of bronchoscopy with transbronchial biopsy. 
      Bilateral pneumothorax developing after BAL without biopsy has been rarely 
      described in the literature. A 51-year-old woman presented with symptoms 
      suggestive of reactive airway syndrome and underwent bronchoscopy with BAL to 
      rule out vocal cord paralysis and to investigate other potential causes of her 
      symptoms. Immediately after BAL, she developed bilateral pneumothorax requiring 
      chest tube placement. The pneumothorax was resolved with the chest tube and the 
      patient recovered. However, the etiology of the pneumothorax remained unclear. We 
      presume that cough-related increase in intrathoracic pressure might have led to 
      interstitial air dissection and bilateral pneumothorax.
