TI  - Bronchoscopy during non-invasive mechanical ventilation: a review of techniques 
      and procedures.
PG  - 105-12
LID - S0300-2896(12)00206-2 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.arbres.2012.05.008 [doi]
AB  - Non-invasive mechanical ventilation (NIMV) is used to treat acute respiratory 
      failure by improving gas exchange abnormalities and reducing the signs of 
      respiratory effort, dyspnea and the activity of accessory respiratory muscles. 
      Bronchoscopy is a key technique in the study of respiratory diseases that is 
      necessary to perform in acute and critical patients, often times only after 
      orotracheal intubation (OTI) due to possible complications of the technique. In 
      this review, we evaluate the evidence of NIMV use during bronchoscopy, concluding 
      that its use should be considered in severe patients as an alternative that is 
      capable of preventing the complications related with OTI and mechanical 
      ventilation, especially in patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease 
      and with a tendency towards developing hypercapnia.
