VI  - 35
IP  - 1
DP  - 2011
TI  - [Cystic echinococcosis mimicking tuberculosis in childhood].
PG  - 53-6
LID - 10.5152/tpd.2011.14 [doi]
AB  - Both cystic echinococcosis (CE) and tuberculosis (Tbc) are important health 
      problems in developing countries. Pulmonary CE and Tbc have to be differentiated 
      from other diseases as they have increased the risk of morbidity and mortality. 
      Besides, these two diseases can mimic each other. Here, we discuss a 7 year-old 
      patient admitted with fever, cough who was unresponsive to nonspecific antibiotic 
      treatment given for pneumonia, had Tbc treatment due to a positive tuberculin 
      skin test and radiologic appearance consistent with Tbc and on follow-up. He was 
      eventually diagnosed as CE based on a cystic lesion consistent with CE in the 
      liver and echinococcosis specific IgE positivity and was succesfully treated with 
      anti-parasitic therapy.
