DP  - 2010 Jun
TI  - Bronchoscopic lung volume reduction in a single-lung transplant recipient with 
      natal lung hyperinflation: a case report.
PG  - 1979-81
LID - 10.1016/j.transproceed.2009.12.071 [doi]
AB  - After single lung transplantation for emphysema native lung hyperinflation is a 
      common complication that may cause respiratory failure. Herein we have reported 
      satisfactory bronchoscopic lung volume reduction in a left single-lung transplant 
      recipient with native lung hyperinflation, who suffered from Medical Research 
      Council (MRC) class 3 dyspnea and chest pain. Three endobronchial valves (Zephyr; 
      Emphasys Medical, Redwood, Calif, United States) were placed into the segmental 
      bronchi of the right upper lobe, using videobronchoscopy under general 
      anesthesia. Postoperative chest computed tomography revealed subsegmental 
      atelectasis in that lobe. The clinical benefit was an improved MRC dyspnea class 
      from 3 to 2, which was still present at 4 months after the procedure, although 
      there were no remarkable changes in spirometric parameters.
