VI  - 130
IP  - 7
DP  - 2010 Jul
TI  - Clinical aspects and treatment of primary tracheal malignancies.
PG  - 763-72
LID - 10.3109/00016480903403005 [doi]
AB  - CONCLUSIONS: Selecting patients that are candidates for surgical treatment is 
      important in the work-up of patients with tracheal cancer. Toward this goal, 
      centralization of care concerning tracheal tumors is advised. Centralization may 
      increase long-term survival and decrease operative morbidity and mortality even 
      further. OBJECTIVE: Primary tracheal tumors pose a diagnostic and therapeutic 
      challenge for the physician when confronted with this mostly malignant tumor. 
      Diagnosis is often delayed for months or years due to its aspecific and 
      asthma-mimicking symptoms. Knowledge from retrospective series is limited and few 
      clinicians have gained experience with this tumor. The available literature on 
      the diagnosis and management of this group of tumors is reviewed to summarize the 
      available knowledge about these uncommon tumors. New diagnostic, staging, and 
      treatment guidelines are proposed. METHODS: PubMed was searched for English 
      publications from 1960. The available literature was reviewed and summarized. 
      RESULTS: Surgical resection and primary reconstruction is the best curative 
      treatment modality available at present. In centers of experience, more than half 
      of all patients with tracheal cancer may be candidates for surgical resection, 
      although in population-based studies this treatment is applied in only 10-25% of 
      patients.
